= Intro

As the world enters the period of economic boom, Germany has recovered from the hyperinflation and has stood up to its feet. The current cabinet consists of mostly conservative parties, forming a right-wing coalition of the following:

Zentrum, BVP, DVP and DNVP.

As of now, Interior Minister of Germany and member of the DNVP, Walther Keudell, has been unsuccessful in his attempts to push through the controversial "School Law", which would guarantee equality between interdenominational, confessional and secular schools. Despite support from the religiously conservative BVP, Centrum and DNVP, the liberal DVP has been unwilling to let religious schools be empowered, having both first and revisited drafts struck down by the DVP. This has considerably strained relations between them, and it seems like their cabinet won't last further than a year.

And as the cabinet suffers from its inability to push the "School of Law", the German right (especially the grassroots) has entered a period of division: WP (Economic Party of the Middle Class) and VRP (Inflation revaluation party), as the parties representing mostly Mittelstand and Unemployed people, have been assaulting classic right parties for "abandoning middle class" and burdening their demographics, further siphoning liberal and nationalist votes. It was as though the quiet misdirections of the Marx and Luther governments had finally summoned a storm long gathering on the horizon...

---

As the German right have failed the nation, could Social Democrats, perhaps, grab this opportunity and finally be given a chance to form a government?

- @intro_2: What about us?

@intro_2

This year, we, Social Democrats (SPD), have secured marginal victories: We have pushed the bill for National Unemployment Insurance, state intervention in the bargaining process through binding arbitration, expansion of factory councils and general improvement in quality of life amongst blue collar workers. Our image has improved amongst those who we have once alienated with failure of early Weimar years. Our members are optimistic enough that they believe we could **enter the next government**, however our majority is cautious about entering the government and responsibilities behind this considering our history with bourgeouise parties. Perhaps, we could aim for Weimar Coalition?

